Diagnosis: all five failures are semantic/grounding errors, not SQL syntax errors — literal values guessed instead of read from the DB (`'Korea '`, `'Bachelors'`, exact-title match), wrong projection breadth ("details"/"which countries" → gold returns whole rows), and a negation query that silently returns 0 rows. The incumbent harness never uses `env.execute` or `env.descriptions`, so the model gets no chance to notice an empty result or to see real cell values. I add (1) a value-grounded prompt with explicit rules for literals, "details" projections, set-difference negation and ties, (2) column descriptions in the user message, and (3) a draft→execute→repair loop that only spends the second policy call when the draft errors or returns zero rows, feeding back real sample cell values.

